SCOPE OF THE LICENSE GRANT

The Apache-2.0 grant above covers only the material authored under Trailmap/:
the specifications in specs/, the research notes in research/, the tools in
tools/, and the accompanying prose and data.

It does NOT extend to, and this repository does not redistribute:

  * SSX Tricky, its executables, disc contents, level data, textures, audio, or
    any other retail game asset.
  * Any trademark of Electronic Arts Inc.

Files derived from retail game data are generated locally by the user from
their own copy of the game and are excluded from version control. See the
"Local-only inputs" section of README.md.

The research/ directory and the citation definitions under specs/ carry addresses,
field offsets, analyst-assigned function labels, retail identifiers, and short
instruction quotations -- usually a single instruction, sometimes a short sequence,
never a listing -- kept as evidence for the claims they support. No bulk disassembly listing or retail
configuration/script passage is reproduced anywhere under Trailmap/; configuration
and script references retain only field names and short fragments needed to identify
an interface. Unless expressly marked as a retail symbol, RTTI name, or string-table
entry, a `Subsystem_Verb` function name is an analyst's description. The
patch generators under tools/patches/ and the probes under tools/instrumentation/ and
tools/autotest/ carry no encoded instruction words: they recognise a build and verify a
site by the digest of what it holds. Where a hook must re-execute the instruction its
branch displaced, a probe reads that word from the running emulator at install and checks
it by digest before building around it, and a patch generator names it by mnemonic and
source address -- the same short quotation the citations carry -- and emits a directive
naming where to copy that word from in your own executable rather than the word itself;
the probes patch emulator memory only and produce no patched executable and no patch
definition — what they write is capture and recovery data on your own machine. The Apache grant applies
only to OpenSlope-authored
selection, arrangement, commentary, tools, and expression; it does not purport to
relicense any quoted third-party expression. Implementations consume specs/, not
those citations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RETAIL-DERIVED FACTS

A bounded class of retail-derived facts is carried in the tree itself: format
and asset identifiers, enumeration and bank names, and measured functional
constants. The authoritative data copies for the audio bank catalog and the
per-surface response table are specs/data/external-sound-banks-v1.json and
specs/data/ride-v1.json; each downstream component's NOTICE names the further
retail-derived facts its own source carries. These are the functional interface
implementations interoperate through, retained because interoperating requires
them. Beyond them, the research notes and citation definitions quote measured
values -- thresholds, frame tables, per-surface terms -- as evidence for the
claims they support, and nothing more: no retail code, asset, or executable
accompanies any of it, and everything else is generated locally from your own
copy of the game and stays out of version control.

